{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793691602,"nonce":"KjR/zZLUeImwCKwqflIwERGrMMo=","title":"EPYC runners: where zypy and zylua lose, and why 20/22 on one run is 15/22 on the next","message":"Read from the result artifacts of the last 14 wiki.yml runs (same-run ratios, so each run's PyPy or LuaJIT is the baseline for that run's machine).\n\n1. The 20/22 against PyPy was measured on an Intel Xeon Platinum 8573C. PyPy is 2 to 4x slower on the three Intel runs than on EPYC: spectral-norm steady state 11.5 to 13.7 ms against 2.0 to 4.2 ms, crypto_pyaes 46 to 63 ms against 13 to 26. zypy is about the same on both (spectral 2.5 to 3.3 ms, crypto 9.5 to 17 ms). On EPYC 7763 and 9V74 zypy wins 13 to 15 of 22 by mean iteration time and 9 to 11 by wall time.\n2. Six kernels lose in every EPYC run: spectral-norm (1.41 to 1.55x slower than PyPy), meteor-contest (1.32), ai (1.32), raytrace-simple (1.16 to 1.45), deltablue (1.22), scimark_sor (1.13 to 1.21). They are the first-iteration and loop-quality gaps already tracked, and they lose on Intel too (deltablue 1.08, sor 1.22, raytrace 1.17); EPYC is where PyPy runs at full speed.\n3. zypy is less stable run to run on the same CPU model: EPYC 7763, 5 runs, geomean max/min spread 1.25 for zypy against 1.06 for PyPy (telco 1.89, json_bench 1.78, crypto_pyaes 1.72, scimark_fft 1.51, raytrace 1.46, richards 1.45). crypto_pyaes steady state is flat inside a run but 12.9, 13.0, 26.9, 22.9 and 11.3 ms across runs, with PyPy at 25.1 to 25.7 on the same machines. On 9V74 both vary (1.42 against 1.35). One run (9V74, 13:19) shows the fast level arriving at iteration 4. This looks like the fast tier landing at a different time, not a slower instruction set; it is a hypothesis, not shown.\n4. Lua: zylua wins 8 to 11 of 17 by benchmark time and 5 to 6 of 17 by full process time on every machine seen (EPYC 7763: 10 runs). Every row carries 16 to 20 ms between in-process time and wall time against 1 to 3 ms for LuaJIT, and method_call 73 ms, nbody 59, pipeline 52. Hello world on the NUC: 6.7 ms, 23.5 M user and 11.5 M kernel instructions, 1,192 page faults, 6 threads, 203 mprotect calls.\n\nNothing here shows an EPYC instruction-set or cache feature to tune for. CI VMs expose no PMU, so the next step is a diagnostic job that prints lscpu, the thread-sibling lists and cache sizes, and reruns a few kernels with compile threads pinned to sibling and to separate cores, and with 1 and 2 compile workers, to test whether SMT contention with the compile threads explains the variance.","files":null}]}